  <info><![CDATA[<font color="#D73229" size="8"><b>Plague in Tadrar (v2)</b></font>

*A Plague Inc-style epidemic set in a real Berber village, grounded in Crawford (2008). One tick = one day; a run lasts a year.*

## What it is

How do a disease’s traits - how catchy, how deadly, how sneaky - mix with Tadrar’s communal labor (**khams**) to shape an epidemic, & what the shock does to households?

**Plague Inc** (a game, Ndemic Creations 2012): you design a disease. Its lesson is a trade-off - kill too fast & you get noticed & burn out; stay quiet & you spread everywhere. Epidemiologists call this the virulence-transmission trade-off (Anderson & May 1982, first fit to myxomatosis).

**Tadrar**: a Berber village in Morocco’s High Atlas that David Crawford (2008) studied; he counted **28 households**. Families farm their own unequal fields but all owe labor to **khams** - communal work, above all cleaning the irrigation canal. Khams is a shared-resource (commons) problem, like the Balinese water temples Lansing & Kremer (1993) modeled: it only works if enough households turn out. It is also where different households mix, so it’s how disease jumps between them. (The real khams is a five-part division of the village that owes a fifth of the labor while owning less; I fold it into “every house owes a worker,” & leave out the 9-10 day water turns.)

## The valley

Mountains on the edges, houses on the dry slopes, green fields along the river, the canal (light blue) across the top, & the khams site (purple) by the canal.

**Households** (houses) have one field each, a grain store, & an unequal amount of **land** - most hold a little, a few hold a lot - so you can see Crawford’s inequality at a glance (houses are sized by their land). **Villagers** belong to a house; about 60% are workers, & a share **migrate** for wage labor in the city - Crawford’s theme, & Prof. West’s own Sahel research (West 2009).

## How a day works

**1. Work.** Workers (those not away for wage labor) head to their field; every few days each house sends one to khams. Sick people stay home.
**2. Spread.** Anyone infected can infect people right next to them - at work, & again at home.
**3. Live or die.** Farmers bring in grain (more land means more, less if the canal is poor), everyone eats one, & the disease runs hidden → sick → immune or dead. A hungry house is weaker, so its sick are 1.5× likelier to die.

Underneath it all: the **canal** decays & khams repairs it; deaths past a threshold set off an **alarm** so scared houses skip khams; a **cure** bar fills; & the disease can **mutate** to deadly on a set day.

**Colors:** white healthy, red hidden, orange sick, gray immune. Houses are sized by land, turn yellow when out of grain, & fields brown when the canal fails.

## How to run it

**Pick a preset, then press `go`.** (The village sliders - num-households, khams-interval, withdrawal, migration, & initial-infected - you can set first; the disease dials are set by the presets.)

* **Burnout plague** - deadly & obvious; spreads through a few houses, then burns out.
* **Silent spreader** - mild & sneaky; infects most of the valley, kills few.
* **Plague Inc strategy** - silent, then mutates to deadly on day 20.

## Experiments to try

Each changes one thing & watches what happens - & each hits one part of my question (how a disease’s traits mix with khams to shape the epidemic, & what the shock does to households).

**Compare the three presets.** Run burnout, silent spreader, & Plague Inc. Burnout kills hard but reaches few (~22% infected); the silent strain spreads everywhere (~62%); Plague Inc does both & kills the most (~66 deaths). The virulence-transmission trade-off, straight out of the model.

**Sweep the khams-interval slider.** This is the heart of my question - *is the communal labor that keeps the canal alive also spreading the sickness?* Hold the silent strain fixed & change only how often the village does khams. The answer is a clean, steady climb: khams every 7 days infects ~18% of the valley, every 3 days ~71%, every day ~94%. The more the village gathers, the more the disease jumps between households. Khams is the main route the disease travels - a no-khams control (on my list below) would pin it down for sure.

**Sweep the lethality slider.** There is a **threshold**, not a smooth slope. At low lethality the strain reaches ~42% of the valley; once it is deadly enough to kill about 3 people it trips the **alarm**, & past that point the size drops to a noisy ~15-30% & stops climbing. It is not host burnout (the infectious period is fixed here) - the cap comes from the village’s own khams-withdrawal kicking in sooner against a deadlier strain (the cure bar fills far too slowly to matter here). No single rule sets the cap; it falls out of the alarm & withdrawal interacting.

**Withdrawal 0 vs 100.** Run Plague Inc with withdrawal low, then high. Watch the **canal**: pulling households out of khams barely changes the death toll, but the canal slips (about 85 down to 57) & every harvest suffers - a commons breaking down.

**Migration 0 vs 50.** More men away means less labor, but also fewer people exposed - so the epidemic spreads less.

**Run the same preset a few times.** It’s random: some outbreaks fizzle in the first house, others take the valley.

## What I did (my extension)

I started from the class **Spread of Disease** & **Flocking** models & took them further:

* made the disease play like **Plague Inc** - stealth, lethality, a cure bar, mutation, & presets
* set it in Crawford’s **Tadrar** - 28 households on unequal land, fields, a canal, & khams labor
* added **grain, migration, & a hunger feedback**, so I can track inequality (the grain spread) over the year, the way West & Crawford study household livelihoods

**The surprise:** I expected the epidemic to *widen* inequality (Crawford calls Tadrar’s inequality “temporal”). The honest answer is subtler, & it only shows up if you measure inequality the right way. The raw grain gap does end *lower* with the plague (~285 vs ~403 in a normal year) - but that is only because the plague shrinks the whole harvest (average grain falls from ~293 to ~199). Measured relative to the average - the scale-free **coefficient of variation** (gap ÷ average), which is how inequality is actually defined - the plague does *not* equalize the village; CV holds about flat, if anything edging up (~1.37 to ~1.43). So the plague doesn’t level Tadrar, it just makes everyone poorer. Inequality here is set by unequal **land**, not by the plague.

## Limits & what I’d do next

Things I simplified & what I’d add if I had more time:

* **Khams is stripped down.** The real khams is a five-part division that owes a fifth of the labor; I fold it into “every house sends a worker,” & I leave out the 9-10 day water turns.
* **One village, fixed land.** Land never changes hands & there’s no household life-cycle, so I can’t really show Crawford’s *temporal* inequality - the way a household rises & falls over a generation.
* **Migration is one-shot.** People leave once at setup & never send money home or return; West’s households move back & forth.
* **What I’d add next:** a real Gini measure, error bars on the sweeps, & a no-khams control to pin down exactly how much of the spread is the communal labor.

## The NetLogo I used

The model is mine, but not the language. Where each non-obvious command came from:

* **my turtle-grass lab** - `ask`, `if`/`ifelse`, `hatch`, `die`, `random`, `create-<breed>`
* **my Game of Life lab** - `patches-own`, `count ... with [...]`, nested `ifelse`
* **class Spread of Disease model** - `one-of`, `n-of`, `any?`, `with`, `myself`, `self`
* **class Flocking model** - `in-radius`, `to-report`
* **NetLogo Programming Guide / Dictionary** - `breed`, `mod`, `standard-deviation`, `random-exponential`

Anything I was unsure of I checked in BIND (bind.netlogo.org) & the NetLogo docs (docs.netlogo.org/7.0.3).

## Credits & References (Sources)

Chigozie Nnani <a href="https:/chigoziennani.com">**(chigoziennani.com)**</a> - ANTH 404 - Fall 2026

**Ideas & setting**

* Crawford, D. (2008). *Moroccan Households in the World Economy: Labor and Inequality in a Berber Village.* Baton Rouge: LSU Press. *(Tadrar; 28 households; land/labor inequality; khams & the canal)*
* Anderson, R. M., & May, R. M. (1982). Coevolution of hosts and parasites. *Parasitology, 85*(2), 411-426. *(the virulence-transmission trade-off; myxomatosis)*
* Lansing, J. S., & Kremer, J. N. (1993). Emergent properties of Balinese water temple networks. *American Anthropologist, 95*(1), 97-114. *(irrigation as an emergent commons - the khams/canal framing)*
* West, C. T. (2009). Domestic transitions, desiccation, agricultural intensification, and livelihood diversification among rural households on the Central Plateau, Burkina Faso. *American Anthropologist, 111*(3), 275-288. *(household livelihoods, diversification, & migration)*
* Ndemic Creations. (2012). *Plague Inc.* [Video game]. *(the pathogen design: sliders, the cure bar, & mutation)*

**Code & NetLogo**

* Wilensky, U. (2008). NetLogo Spread of Disease model; (1998) Flocking; (1999) NetLogo. Center for Connected Learning, Northwestern University.
* Wilensky, U., & Rand, W. (2015). *An Introduction to Agent-Based Modeling.* MIT Press.
* Beginner’s Interactive NetLogo Dictionary (BIND, 2021); NetLogo Programming Guide + Dictionary v7.0.3.
]]></info>
